# YewReview — a local agent that writes market research reports.
# Copy to .env. Every YewReview setting is YEWREVIEW_-prefixed; see the warning at the bottom for why.

# Server
YEWREVIEW_HOST=127.0.0.1
YEWREVIEW_PORT=8787

# Runtime root: the database, the agent's home directory, the chart library published reports load
# from, the sandbox venv, and the caches. One root per installation. There is no data tree and no
# directory per recipe — a report is a row, content and all, and a recipe is a record rather
# than a place.
#
# Left COMMENTED OUT on purpose, because the right default differs between the two ways of running
# and setting it here would flatten them into one. A checkout uses ./var beside the source; the
# installed binary uses ~/.yewreview, because it is run from wherever your shell happens to be and a
# cwd-relative root would scatter a database and a venv across every directory you ever called it
# from. Uncommenting this pins BOTH to one path — including the installed command, which reads this
# file whenever it is started from this directory. Set it only to make a second, separate instance.
# YEWREVIEW_VAR_DIR=var

# Agent
YEWREVIEW_MODEL=claude-opus-5
YEWREVIEW_MAX_TURNS=100

# The wall-clock ceiling, in milliseconds, on any single command the agent runs through the one
# door — a stored script, a measurement, or an ad-hoc line. Past it the process is KILLED and the
# archive gets the exit code that kill produced; the timeout itself never reaches the run tables,
# and what says so is the answer the tool hands back — `run_shell` reporting "the command exited N
# (timed out)" and `run_script` naming the script the same way. Both halves, because a run that was
# cut off produced no answer and the exit code alone reads as an ordinary failure. Ten minutes by
# default, which is generous on purpose: a full-grid measurement over a long index legitimately
# takes minutes and the first one on a machine pays the engine's compilation on top. It is here so
# a script waiting on a socket nobody is answering cannot hold the conversation open forever, not
# to police how long honest work takes.
#
# Do not leave it present but empty. This is the one setting read straight off the environment
# rather than through the trim every other YEWREVIEW_ name gets, so a blank value is not "unset"
# but the number ZERO — a ceiling every command breaches as it starts, killing all of them.
# YEWREVIEW_RUN_TIMEOUT_MS=600000

# How much the server says for itself: debug, info, warning, error. Defaults to info; debug adds the
# agent's own frame-by-frame account, which is a great deal of output.
# YEWREVIEW_LOG_LEVEL=

# The interpreter used to build the sandbox venv, handed to `uv venv --python`. Leave unset and uv
# resolves one itself, downloading a suitable Python when the machine has none.
# The measurement engine requires Python 3.13 or newer.
# YEWREVIEW_PYTHON=

# An installed `claude` executable to use instead of the one compiled into the binary — an escape
# hatch for a platform the SDK publishes no executable for. The build embeds the one for the machine
# it runs on, so this is only needed somewhere that has neither.
# YEWREVIEW_CLAUDE_CLI=

# The same, for opencode. The binary carries the pinned version for the machine that built it, so
# this is for a platform opencode publishes nothing for — or for an extracted copy something on the
# machine refuses to run.
# YEWREVIEW_OPENCODE_CLI=

# And for uv, which the binary also carries and which provisions the measurement environment. A dev
# checkout has no embedded copy and uses whatever is on PATH; this overrides either.
# YEWREVIEW_UV=

# Leave this COMMENTED OUT if you use Claude Code on a Claude subscription: the agent falls back to
# the login already on this machine (`claude setup-token`, or CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN), and that is
# the whole setup. Uncomment it only to bill against the API instead.
#
# Do not leave it present but empty. An empty value shadows the login instead of falling through to
# it, which fails at the first turn and looks like a broken install rather than a blank line.
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# Credentials for data sources live in ordinary environment variables with any name that is neither
# YEWREVIEW_- nor SEIKAN_-prefixed, for example:
# EXAMPLE_API_KEY=...
# An information source records only the variable's NAME, in its auth_env field; the value stays
# here and is read by the agent's scripts as $NAME. It never enters the database.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NEVER name a YewReview setting SEIKAN_*.
# The measurement engine owns that entire environment-variable namespace for its four gate
# thresholds, and its settings model REFUSES to construct when it meets any SEIKAN_* variable it
# does not recognise — a hard failure reported as `thresholds_invalid`, not a warning, and one that
# would fire on every single run. YewReview scrubs SEIKAN_* out of the environment at startup, before
# anything else reads it, and passes thresholds only as explicit arguments.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
